CAT = "ccrnNeuro"; SEG = "NEU"
Q = [
("After a tibial fracture is casted, which finding is the earliest sign of compartment syndrome?",
 "Pain on passive stretch", ["An absent distal pulse", "Pale, cool skin on the foot", "Paralysis of the foot"],
 "Pain out of proportion to the injury and pain when the muscles of the compartment are passively stretched come first. Pulses often stay present until late, and paralysis means damage may already be permanent."),
("How should a limb with suspected compartment syndrome be positioned while the surgeon is called?",
 "Level with the heart", ["Raised well above the heart", "Hanging below the heart", "Wrapped firmly in elastic"],
 "Raising the limb lowers arterial inflow and can worsen ischemia, so it is kept at heart level with tight dressings or casts loosened. Fasciotomy is the definitive treatment."),
("Two days after a femur fracture, a patient becomes confused and hypoxemic with petechiae across the chest and axillae. What is most likely?",
 "Fat embolism syndrome", ["A pulmonary contusion", "A tension pneumothorax", "Alcohol withdrawal"],
 "Respiratory distress, neurologic changes and petechiae 24 to 72 hours after a long bone or pelvic fracture are the classic triad of fat embolism syndrome. Early fixation of fractures lowers the risk."),
("A patient with a new cervical spinal cord injury is hypotensive. Which findings point to neurogenic shock rather than hemorrhage?",
 "Bradycardia with warm, dry skin", ["Tachycardia with cool, pale skin", "Tachycardia with warm skin", "Bradycardia with cold, mottled skin"],
 "Loss of sympathetic tone below the injury dilates vessels and leaves vagal tone unopposed, so the patient is hypotensive, bradycardic and warm. Hemorrhage causes tachycardia and cool, pale skin."),
("A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury has a pounding headache, BP 210/110, flushing above the injury and a heart rate of 48. What should the nurse do first?",
 "Sit them up; check the bladder", ["Lay them flat, legs raised", "Give a fast IV fluid bolus", "Apply a warming blanket now"],
 "This is autonomic dysreflexia, triggered by a stimulus below the injury, most often a full bladder or bowel. Sitting the patient up lowers BP while the cause is found and removed; antihypertensives follow if it persists."),
("A spinal cord injury at which level most threatens diaphragm function?",
 "C3 to C5", ["T1 to T4", "T10 to T12", "L1 to L3"],
 "The phrenic nerve arises from C3, C4 and C5, so injuries at or above these levels can paralyze the diaphragm and need ventilatory support."),
("A patient with a head injury has a MAP of 80 mm Hg and an ICP of 20 mm Hg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure?",
 "60 mm Hg", ["100 mm Hg", "4 mm Hg", "40 mm Hg"],
 "CPP = MAP - ICP, so 80 - 20 = 60 mm Hg. Adding the two gives 100 and dividing gives 4; both are common slips."),
("Which set of findings is Cushing's triad of rising intracranial pressure?",
 "Bradycardia, wide pulse pressure", ["Tachycardia, narrow pulse pressure", "Tachycardia and hypotension", "Bradycardia and hypotension"],
 "Late in rising ICP, systolic pressure climbs with a widening pulse pressure, the heart rate slows and breathing becomes irregular. It signals brainstem compression and needs immediate action."),
("Where is the transducer of an external ventricular drain usually leveled?",
 "At the tragus of the ear", ["At the phlebostatic axis", "At the iliac crest", "At the sternal notch"],
 "The tragus approximates the foramen of Monro, the zero point for ICP. Leveling at the heart's phlebostatic axis is for hemodynamic lines and would give a false ICP and wrong drainage."),
("A patient has an external ventricular drain open to drainage. What should the nurse do before moving the patient up in bed?",
 "Clamp the drain", ["Open the drain fully", "Flush the catheter", "Lower the drip chamber"],
 "Clamping before a change in head height prevents a sudden over-drainage of CSF, which can cause ventricular collapse or bleeding. The drain is releveled and reopened right after the move."),
("A patient with an aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage is given oral nimodipine. Why?",
 "To improve neurologic outcome", ["To dissolve the clot faster", "To raise the blood pressure", "To prevent early seizures"],
 "Nimodipine improves outcome after aneurysmal SAH and is given for 21 days; it can lower BP, which is watched. It does not treat the aneurysm or prevent seizures."),
("When is cerebral vasospasm most likely after an aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage?",
 "Days 3 to 14", ["The first 6 hours", "After 30 days", "Hours 6 to 12"],
 "Vasospasm usually starts around day 3 and peaks around days 7 to 10, so new focal deficits or a falling level of consciousness in that window need urgent evaluation."),
("A patient arrives within the time window for stroke thrombolysis. Which imaging must be done before the drug is given?",
 "A noncontrast head CT", ["A lumbar puncture", "A carotid ultrasound", "A transthoracic echo"],
 "A noncontrast CT (or MRI) rules out hemorrhage, which is a contraindication to thrombolysis. Lumbar puncture, carotid and cardiac studies come later and must not delay treatment."),
("A patient with a new stroke asks for oral medications and water. What must happen first?",
 "A swallow screen", ["Crush the tablets in water", "Raise the head of the bed", "A gag reflex check alone"],
 "Dysphagia is common after stroke, so nothing is given by mouth until a swallow screen is passed. A present gag reflex does not prove a safe swallow."),
("A patient has a generalized tonic-clonic seizure lasting 6 minutes with IV access in place. Which drug is given first?",
 "IV lorazepam", ["IV phenytoin", "IV propofol", "IV haloperidol"],
 "A benzodiazepine is first-line for status epilepticus; a second-line drug such as fosphenytoin, levetiracetam or valproate follows if seizures continue. Propofol is reserved for refractory status."),
("Which condition must be corrected before a brain death examination is done?",
 "Hypothermia", ["Fixed pupils", "Absent cough reflex", "Absent corneal reflex"],
 "Hypothermia, hypotension, sedating drugs and severe metabolic disturbances can mimic loss of brain function, so they are corrected before testing. Fixed pupils and absent brainstem reflexes are findings of the examination itself."),
("A week after a severe TBI, a patient has episodes of fever, tachycardia, hypertension, sweating and posturing with no infection found. What is most likely?",
 "Sympathetic storming", ["Early septic shock", "Neurogenic shock", "Thyroid storm"],
 "Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity, or storming, follows severe brain injury and causes bursts of sympathetic overactivity. It is treated by removing triggers and with drugs such as beta blockers and opioids."),
("A patient is admitted with suspected bacterial meningitis caused by Neisseria meningitidis. Which precautions are used?",
 "Droplet precautions", ["Airborne precautions", "Contact precautions", "Standard precautions"],
 "Meningococcal disease spreads by respiratory droplets, so droplet precautions are kept until 24 hours of effective antibiotics. Close contacts may need prophylaxis."),
("After a head strike, a patient is briefly knocked out, then talks normally, then rapidly becomes unresponsive with a dilated pupil. What is most likely?",
 "An epidural hematoma", ["A chronic subdural hematoma", "A mild concussion", "Diffuse axonal injury"],
 "A lucid interval followed by rapid decline is typical of arterial bleeding into the epidural space, often from the middle meningeal artery. It needs emergency evacuation."),
("For routine care of a ventilated patient with a severe TBI, what PaCO2 range is usually kept?",
 "35 to 45 mm Hg", ["20 to 25 mm Hg", "50 to 60 mm Hg", "25 to 30 mm Hg"],
 "Normal PaCO2 is kept because hyperventilation constricts cerebral vessels and can cause ischemia; it is used only briefly for impending herniation. A high PaCO2 dilates vessels and raises ICP."),
("A patient with a ventriculoperitoneal shunt has a headache, vomiting and increasing drowsiness. What should the nurse suspect?",
 "Shunt malfunction", ["A migraine headache", "Viral gastroenteritis", "Low blood glucose"],
 "Headache, vomiting and a falling level of consciousness in a patient with a shunt suggest blockage and rising ICP until proven otherwise. Imaging and neurosurgical review are urgent."),
("How should a patient with an unstable thoracic spine fracture be turned?",
 "Log roll with the team", ["Sit up and dangle", "Pull up using a trapeze", "Lift by the arms"],
 "Log rolling keeps the head, shoulders and hips in line so the spine does not twist. A trapeze, sitting up or pulling on the arms flexes or rotates the spine."),
("A ventilated patient on a stable low dose of norepinephrine is alert and following commands. What does the PADIS guideline say about mobilizing this patient?",
 "Mobilize if otherwise stable", ["Wait until extubated", "Wait until off all pressors", "Passive range of motion only"],
 "The 2018 PADIS guideline suggests rehabilitation and mobilization and notes that mechanical ventilation and vasoactive infusions are not barriers when the patient is otherwise stable. New instability is a reason to stop."),
("A malnourished patient with alcohol use disorder is confused with an unsteady gait and abnormal eye movements. Which order of treatment is correct?",
 "Thiamine before glucose", ["Glucose before thiamine", "Folic acid alone", "Naloxone before thiamine"],
 "These signs suggest Wernicke encephalopathy. Thiamine is given before or with glucose, because a glucose load uses up the remaining thiamine and can worsen the injury."),
]
